  "notes": [
    "`Pressure` read in hectopascals, from the values: the first row's, 877.2, lies within the 500 to 1100 hPa of any launch site, and the highest, 877.2, within the 10 to 1200 mbar the RRC3 measures (RRC3 manual p. 9)",
    "`Altitude` read in feet, from the pressure: from line 2 to line 17 the pressure falls from 877.20 hPa to 853.70 hPa, a climb of 222.3 m in the U.S. Standard Atmosphere (1976), and the altitude climbs 727.20 as written, -0.28% off it read in feet and +227.2% read in meters",
    "`Velocity` read in miles per hour, from the altitude: over 52 rows, against the altitude above the first row, in meters, as it was 0.00 s earlier, the area under its running integral is 2.1966 times the altitude's, and the integral's least-squares slope is 2.2107; miles per hour would give 2.2369 per m/s, -1.80% off the first and -1.17% off the second; the next nearest, feet per second, would miss them by -33.0% and -32.6%",
    "`Time` read in seconds, assumed: the median step between rows, 0.5 as written, isn't within 5% of the 0.05 s of the 20 Hz the RRC3 records its pressure at (RRC3 manual p. 23); mDACS's chart is timed in seconds (mDACS manual p. 8)",
    "`Voltages` read in volts, assumed: the RRC3 records its battery's voltage (mDACS manual p. 2) and takes a battery of at most 10 V (RRC3 manual p. 6); its values, as written, run from 8.95 to 9.2",
    "2 rows, the first on line 52, leave out the 1 Hz `Voltages` cell: they are gaps"
  ]
